        "id": "desert-and-mountain",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Two contrasting environments",
        "content": "Desert exploration benefits from a vocabulary more precise than “sand.” In Iranian geographical usage, a dasht can describe a gravel plain or piedmont, while a kavir is a saline playa composed of fine sediments and salts. Recognizing those distinctions changes what a visitor notices: a pale basin floor and a gravel apron below mountains record different processes. [s3]\n\nMountaineering introduces another distinction: technical movement and physiological demands are separate questions. The International Climbing and Mountaineering Federation, known as the UIAA, identifies inadequate acclimatization and overly rapid ascent as important factors in altitude illness. An itinerary’s ascent profile therefore deserves attention alongside its advertised summit or scenic appeal. This is general mountain-health context, not an assessment of any particular Iranian route or traveller. [s4]",
